 "\[LineSeparator]",
 "gives the value of the ordered ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 "-cochain ",
 StyleBox["\[Alpha]", "TI"],
 " on an increasing tuple ",
 StyleBox["v\:2080 < \[Ellipsis] < v\:2096", "TI"],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"Missing", "[", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<NonIncreasingTuple\>\"", ",", " ", "tuple"}], "]"}]], 
  "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "da3a8dc1-9c1b-45c1-b4ba-bca5c3bd72e5"],
 " on any other tuple.",
 "\n"
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"5e668520-8d4f-4132-a324-c1748a1c57b4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Definition: with the vertices in a linear order, an ordered ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 "-cochain is a function on the increasing (",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 " + 1)-tuples of the cliques alone, extended by 0 to the increasing tuples \
that span no clique. The order is the one in which ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["Sort"]], "paclet:ref/Sort"},
   "RefLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "c8e70abf-78ad-4938-b211-b6613ebd848b"],
 " puts the vertex names."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"2171c1e6-2a08-4912-add2-84182eae1017"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Restricting an alternating cochain to the increasing tuples identifies the \
alternating and the ordered ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 "-cochains as vector spaces, one value per ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 "-simplex, so both are stored alike, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"<|", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"v0", ",", " ", "...", ",", " ", "vk"}], "}"}], " ", "->", " ", 
    "value"}], "|>"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6555a9a1-9e71-4aa4-bb91-2aa5c7699a15"],
 ". The identification does not respect the products: extended by the sign \
rule, the ordered cup of two alternating cochains is not their Alexander\
\[Dash]Whitney product on the other orderings. An ordered cochain is \
therefore read on the increasing tuples only."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"3b5c21c2-fff6-49ac-9307-9e65d09c28a1"],

